// @emit-error emitProgram: ref valtype with no interned shape
// `.slice` over an UNDECLARED list whose element has a nested object field matching a
// DECLARED union arm's layout — silent-class-inventory D227, D179's second site.
//
// Same two ingredients as D179: `Cir2` is a union member so `collectS` gives it no `sNames`
// row and nothing interns the nested `{ c2: 1 }` shape, and no OTHER ref list is interned so
// `rlElemKindTbl` / `rlElemName` are EMPTY. `refListSlotOfExpr` clamps its miss to slot `0`
// rather than answering -1, so `ms >= 0` cannot see the miss and `rlElemName[0]` on an empty
// table was an out-of-bounds read INSIDE the compiler: `vl check` rc 0, then
// `wasm trap: out of bounds array access`, with `vl build -o` writing no module.
//
// THIS SITE IS WORSE-PLACED THAN D179's. It trapped inside `refListElemNameOfExpr`, which
// `refListSlotOfExpr` itself calls — before any caller has a slot to bound-test, so D179's
// caller-side guard could not cover it. `.filter` reaches the same read; `for z in
// c.slice(0, 1)` reaches BOTH sites in sequence and needs both guards.
//
// One-line discriminators, all built and run: drop the `.slice` and it is a loud reject;
// `.map` instead of `.slice` is a loud reject; interning any second ref list
// (`const pad: Other[] = [{ o: 1 }]`) is a loud reject; deleting the two `type` lines RUNS
// and prints 1; declaring `Cir2` without making it a union member RUNS and prints 1.
type Cir2 = { c2: i32 }
type Shape2 = Cir2 | i32
const c = [{ r: { c2: 1 } }]
print(c.slice(0, 1)[0].r.c2)
